1 NAME Joen Robertssøn
2 GIVN Joen Robertssøn
2 NICK Jon Robertzon
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE BEF 1645
1 OCCU Borgermester i Trondheim
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Joen Robertsen''' (also written '''Jon Robbertzon''' or '''Jens'''; died before 1645) was a prominent Trondheim magistrate, landowner, customs official, and burgomaster during the first half of the 17th century.
2 CONC  Prior to entering the highest civic governance of Trondheim, he spent a decade serving as the regional bailiff of Namdalen.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT === Origins and Chronological Context ===
2 CONT No direct primary sources have been found detailing his exact birthplace or date of birth, but he was established in Trondheim well before 1606. Based on his patronymic, historians have hypothesized that he may have been a son of 
2 CONC "Robert Skott" (Robert the Scot), a Scottish merchant operating in Trondheim during the opening decades of the 1600s. Joen Robertsen passed away sometime before 1645.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Civic Career: From Regional Bailiff to Burgomaster ===
2 CONT Joen Robertsen held several high-level administrative positions throughout his career:
2 CONT * '''War Witness:''' He is first mentioned in public records on August 13, 1611, when he appended his seal to a formal legal testimony regarding the ongoing war (The Kalmar War).
2 CONT * '''Bailiff of Namdal:''' Beginning in 1612, he is recorded as the royal bailiff (fogd) of Namdalen, a position he maintained for nearly a decade until May 1, 1621.
2 CONT * '''Burghership and Customs Service:''' In 1621, he relocated permanently to Trondheim to manage the household and estate of his father-in-law. Throughout the 1620s, local fief accounts categorize him as an active burgher and inh
2 CONC abitant of the city. He also served a tenure as the customs collector (toller) of Trondheim, explicitly identified with this title in a formal book dedication in 1627.
2 CONT * '''Proxy to the Fief Lord:''' In the fief financial accounts of 1629/30, he acted as the personal proxy and agent for Fief Lord Jens Juell, authorized to collect outstanding financial claims on the governor's behalf.
2 CONT * '''City Council and Burgomastership:''' He is first recorded as an active member of the Trondheim city council on April 3, 1633. He quickly advanced to chief executive status, as an official charter dated December 11, 1633, expl
2 CONC icitly titles him as the city's burgomaster (mayor). He does not appear in council records after this date.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Commercial Ventures and the Bjørøy Estate ===
2 CONT Alongside his public duties, Joen amassed significant personal wealth, landholdings, and industrial interests:
2 CONT * '''Herring Trade:''' During the 1620s, he participated actively in the highly profitable seasonal herring fisheries in Bjugn, alongside other members of the Trondheim patrician elite.
2 CONT * '''Founding the Bjørøy Estate:''' He acquired extensive landed property within his former bailiwick of Namdalen. By 1625, his estates there were valued at a substantial 10 spd (skylddaler). These holdings formed the core of the 
2 CONC historic *Bjørøygodset* (The Bjørøy Estate), which his widow Margrete continued to hold and manage as late as 1661.
2 CONT * '''Sawmills and Timber:''' In 1627, he owned the Statland sawmill in Namdalen. Additionally, on October 29, 1625, he received a royal license (NRR Vol. V, p. 495) to operate a sawmill in Orkdal, as well as a mill at Sinden in Sk
2 CONC ogn, the latter inherited directly from his father-in-law. He also took ownership of his father-in-law's urban townhouse in Trondheim.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Heraldic Seal and House Mark ===
2 CONT In a 1614 legal document, Joen utilized a personalized heraldic seal. The seal displayed a per-pale divided shield: featuring a demi-fleur-de-lis on the dexter side (viewer's left), and a charge resembling a demi-club on the sinis
2 CONC ter side (viewer's right). The inscription around the seal read *Jon Robbertzon*. In later years, he abandoned the armorial shield, utilizing a traditional personal merchant's house mark (bumerke) as his official seal.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Marriage and Family ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Joen Robertsen married twice:
2 CONT
2 CONT He married '''1) before 1606''' to '''Anne Mentzdatter''' (died December 10, 1606). She was buried in the cemetery of Nidaros Cathedral alongside three of their young children. Based on her patronymic, she is highly probable to ha
2 CONC ve been a daughter of the prominent merchant Mentz von Ravensburg and Margrethe Casparsdotter.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married '''2) after 1606''' to '''Margrete Jacobsdatter Falch'''. She was the daughter of the well-known Chief Justice (lagmann) of Trondheim, Jacob Pedersen, and Margrethe Pedersdatter Falch. It was her father's estate that Jo
2 CONC en moved to the city to oversee in 1621, subsequently inheriting his properties and mills. As his widow, she managed the family's expansive Namdalen land assets for many years.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT * Dahl, Svein Tore (2001): ''Embetsmenn i Midt-Norge i tiden 1536-1660'', p. 44 (section "Joen Robertsen"). Derived from the Danish Chancellery Archives, National Archives of Norway (RAO), Gerhard Schønning's historical works, the
2 CONC  Norwegian Royal Registries (NRR), and the local fief/tax accounts of Trondheim Lehn. Documented via {F1F3358D-B3F4-488A-992E-1B821114504D}.jpg.
